// I know this project falls short of my ideals (and others') on many levels.
// But I made it for a real reason, so I want to validate my effort on those
// grounds.

// Contributions:

// I have developed the concept and practical details of what I call the dynamic
// scroll. (Remember, 'scroll' is a noun!) I wanted to combine useful qualities
// & capabilities of video...
//  * image-centric
//  * enables continuous flow/transition/animation of images
//  * single persistent 'space' to refer to
// and text...
//  * user-paced
//  * navigable
//  * linkable
//  * peripheral context
//  * [non-auditory]
// So a lot of my effort was centered around designing and building out this
// new (to my knowledge) interface. I think there's a lot of potential here.
//
// Furthermore, I started looking into how interactive elements could be
// integrated into a dynamic scroll. This is somewhat of a challenge, because
// while these elements really ought to fit into the natural flow of the scroll
// (rather than be little detached applets in boxes), the scroll's flow is
// deliberately supposed to have a simple, navigable linear structure. There is
// the potential of a clash between the linearity of a pre-ordained presentation
// and the nonlinearity of user interaction. I came up with one somewhat crude
// but fairly effective resolution to this. There's a lot more work to be done
// here.
//
// My chosen topic (graph colorings) is not a particularly radical one. As their
// name suggests, graphs have an obvious graphical interpretation and
// practically all discussions of graphs use copious diagrams. I have tried to
// add to this mathematical-visualization discourse in two ways, beyond the
// introduction of the dynamic-scroll presentation style described above:
// 1. While traditional mathematics writing often uses individual pictures of
//    mathematical structures, and may even (in particularly classy cases)
//    show multiple representations of the same structure, it is very rare to
//    the correspondences between multiple representations shown directly.
//    These correspondences are incredibly powerful, if they can be portrayed
//    directly -- every representation shows a different aspect of the
//    underlying structure, so the correspondences show how all these aspects
//    fit together. With correspondences, the whole becomes greater than the sum
//    of its parts. With traditional media, it is difficult to portray these
//    correspondences, but with animation and interactivity, we can do it.
//    [todo]
// 2. [todo, some representation of higher-order mathematical structure]
